    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **no, you should not make anything of the panel being made up entirely of women.** It is not, in itself, a sign of bias, procedural irregularity, or unfairness.\n\nHere is why, along with what you should focus on instead:\n\n---\n\n### Why the Panel’s Gender Composition Is Not a Productive Angle\n\n1. **Both you and the appointed candidate are women**  \n   Because both you and the successful candidate share the same gender, claims or suspicions of sex-based discrimination would lack a legal or logical basis. An all-female panel selecting one female candidate over another does not suggest gender bias.\n\n2. **Demographics in education**  \n   Education, including leadership and district administration, has high female representation. An all-female committee (e.g., district superintendent, HR director, senior principal, union or board representative) is common and routine.\n\n3. **Reputational risk**  \n   Bringing up the gender makeup of the panel is likely to be viewed as reaching for an grievance rather than addressing the merits of the decision. In education and school leadership networks—which are often close-knit—raising this could negatively impact your professional reputation.\n\n---\n\n### What Likely Determined the Decision\n\nWhile your qualifications on paper appear stronger in terms of formal leadership credentials (a master’s degree and five years as deputy principal versus coursework and teaching experience), hiring panels typically evaluate candidates differently once the shortlisting phase is complete:\n\n* **Shortlisting vs. Final Selection:** Formal credentials and minimum requirements qualify you for the shortlist. Once candidates reach the final interviews, decisions are almost always driven by interview performance, stakeholder alignment, leadership vision, and reference checks.\n* **Contextual Fit:** The school or board may have prioritized deep classroom experience, a specific pedagogical approach, or familiarity with that particular school community over administrative tenure.\n* **Internal Dynamics:** The successful candidate may already have been well-known within the school or district, or may have presented a strategic plan that resonated more closely with the panel's immediate priorities.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Follow up on your request for feedback**  \n   Two weeks is a reasonable amount of time to wait, as HR and public-sector administrative processes can move slowly. Send a brief, polite follow-up emphasizing professional growth:\n   > *\"I am following up on my request from [Date] regarding written feedback/reasoning for the principal selection. I value the process and would appreciate any insights the panel can provide to support my continued professional development.\"*\n\n2. **Review jurisdiction-specific public appointment rules (if applicable)**  \n   If this is a public school system in a jurisdiction with statutory public-service recruitment laws (e.g., civil service rules requiring selection based strictly on merit and defined scoring rubrics), you are typically entitled to the official protocol and scoring summary. Wait for the formal documentation before drawing conclusions.\n\n3. **Focus on the substantive criteria**  \n   When you do receive the written explanation, evaluate it strictly on how the panel assessed the candidates against the published criteria, interview competencies, and references.\n",
